  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Significant rain event for northwest Montana through Tuesday \n   morning.\n\n-  Gradual drying trend develops mid-week alongside morning \n   valley fog.\n\n-  80 percent of ensemble solutions depict another weather-maker\n   to impact the Northern Rockies this weekend.\n\nAs anticipated, the upper low has been bringing moderate to heavy\nrain intensities to the region overnight. No changes were made to\nthe forecast. We still have flood watches out for portions of \nnorthwest Montana today to account for the potential of lowland \nflooding in poor drainage areas, rapid rises on streams and \ncreeks, possibly reaching bankfull, and debris fall on roadways. \nThe axis of heavier rainfall will continue to shift northwards \nwith time today. From now through Tuesday morning, between 1 to 2 \ninches of rainfall is expected in those areas. From the Mission \nMountains north to the Glacier Region, the probability for \nexceeding 2 inches ranges from 30% in West Glacier to over 70% in \nthe higher elevations. \n\nShowers will persist on Tuesday mainly across northwest Montana\nwhile other areas will start to moderate. Lingering boundary-layer\nmoisture will support overnight and early morning fog and low\nstratus clouds on Wednesday and possibly Thursday.  \n\nTemperatures will rebound Wednesday through Friday, getting back\nto near seasonal levels for this time of year...70s and 80s for\nthe lower elevations of Idaho. The flow becomes a bit zonal on\nThursday which means that breezy conditions could develop,\ngenerally westerly winds 10 to 20 mph with gusts to 25 mph,\nlocally higher towards the Divide. This could bring choppy\nconditions at area lakes. \n\nEnsemble guidance and the upstream pattern evolution continues to\nfavor another trough pattern this coming weekend. We could be\nlooking at another round of wet weather with possible mountain \nsnow above 7000 feet(40 to 50% chance).",
